From: setsid <13945393+setsid@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] ps3disk: expose every accessible storage region

The PS3 hypervisor divides the internal drive into regions and reports
which of them the guest LPAR may reach. Under Sony's OtherOS that was
exactly one, so ps3stor_probe_access() could take the first accessible
region and ps3disk could expose it as the one block device belonging to
the hypervisor device.

OtherOS++ firmware skips the ACL check and makes every region reachable.
On a PS3 Slim running Evilnat 4.93 that is four: region 0 is the whole
physical drive, regions 1 and 2 belong to GameOS, and region 3 is the one
created for OtherOS. ps3disk still exposes a single region and logs

	4 accessible regions found. Only the first one will be used

so /dev/ps3da is the whole physical drive and the region the machine
actually boots from cannot be reached at all. Choosing the last region
rather than the first, as out of tree patches do, only swaps which region
is lost, and renames it relative to petitboot, whose kernel exposes all
four as ps3da through ps3dd.

Give every exposed region its own gendisk.

Region identity is carried in a per region private structure reachable
from both queue->queuedata and gendisk->private_data, so the request path
never reconstructs the region from the device number and nothing depends
on the minor allocation scheme.

The hypervisor accepts one outstanding command per device, and the device
has one bounce buffer, one command tag and one interrupt. Every region
queue is therefore created from a single blk_mq_tag_set with a queue depth
of one. Queues built from one tag set draw their tags from the same
bitmap, so that single tag is the device wide permit to use the bounce
buffer, held from blk_mq_start_request() until the interrupt handler ends
the request, which it does only after any read data has been copied out.
This is how SCSI serialises several LUNs behind a host with can_queue set
to one. A busy test in ps3disk_queue_rq() defers with BLK_STS_DEV_RESOURCE
rather than corrupting the bounce buffer, should that ever fail to hold.

Names follow the region index rather than a running count, so a region
keeps its name whichever other regions are exposed, and the names agree
with petitboot: ps3da is region 0 and ps3dd is the OtherOS region of a
standard OtherOS++ layout.

Regions are read-only unless selected otherwise. The exception is the
OtherOS region, taken to be the highest numbered exposed region that does
not span the whole drive. Exposing 870 GB of GameOS data as a writable
block device one letter away from the root filesystem is not a reasonable
default; a mistyped mkfs there costs a GameOS reinstall.

Consequences for userland:

  - On a standard OtherOS++ layout the OtherOS region is /dev/ps3dd, not
    /dev/ps3da. Anyone carrying an earlier out of tree region patch must
    update root=, fstab and the bootloader configuration. Mounting by
    LABEL= or UUID= makes the question moot.

  - Regions that were previously invisible now appear, read-only. The
    GameOS cache region can be mounted read-only from Linux, which is a
    convenient way to move files between the two systems.

  - Three module parameters: regions= selects which regions appear,
    writable= forces regions read-write, and otheros_rw=0 turns off the
    read-write default for the detected OtherOS region.

Exposing one block device per accessible region follows T2 SDE's
architecture/powerpc64/package/linux/0010-ps3stor-multiple-regions.patch,
which established the approach. This implementation differs in carrying
the region in private data rather than deriving it from the minor number,
in serialising submission through a shared tag set, in defaulting to
read-only, and in staying inside ps3disk.c: ps3disk computes its region id
locally and never calls ps3stor_read_write_sectors(), so asm/ps3stor.h,
ps3stor_lib.c, ps3rom and ps3flash need no change.

Applies on top of René Rebe's bounce buffer offset fix, patch 0001 in this
series, which is required for any multi-segment transfer to be correct.

Not upstream. Region 0 is only reachable on custom firmware, and the
read-only policy is specific to the OtherOS++ layout.

Based on Linux 6.4.
